# Payload on Workers

Payload runs inside the Next.js app of `apps/web`: the admin is at `/admin`, its REST API at `/api`, and the public site reads the content through the Local API (no HTTP round trip). The database is Cloudflare D1, the media are in R2, and Payload owns the schema: there is no ORM package and no Drizzle file in this project.

## Where things are

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| Payload config: bindings, plugins, upload limits | `apps/web/src/payload.config.ts` |
| Collections and the global | `apps/web/src/collections/`, `apps/web/src/globals/` |
| Page blocks | `apps/web/src/blocks/index.ts`, rendered by `apps/web/src/components/render-blocks.tsx` |
| Hooks that expire the cache | `apps/web/src/hooks/revalidate.ts` |
| Cached reads of the public site | `apps/web/src/lib/queries.ts`, tags in `apps/web/src/lib/cache-tags.ts` |
| Public site | `apps/web/src/app/(frontend)/` |
| Admin and REST routes (generated by Payload) | `apps/web/src/app/(payload)/` |
| Migrations and their snapshots | `apps/web/src/migrations/` |
| Admin scripts | `apps/web/scripts/` |

The admin group `(payload)` and the site group `(frontend)` each render their own `<html>`: the project has no `src/app/layout.tsx`. Payload's `layout.tsx`, `page.tsx`, API route and `importMap.js` belong to Payload; the config is imported through `@/payload.config` (the `@payload-config` alias of Payload's template fails the import checks of this repository).

## Content model

- **Pages** (`pages`): title, slug, `layout` made of blocks (hero, rich text, media, call to action), SEO fields, `publishedAt`. The page with the slug `home` is served at `/`.
- **Posts** (`posts`): title, slug, excerpt, cover, rich text, SEO fields, `publishedAt`. Listed at `/posts`.
- **Media** (`media`): images with a required `alt`. `focalX` and `focalY` (percent) say where an image is framed, because Workers cannot crop (see `media.md`).
- **Users** (`users`): editors. Every user has access to the whole admin.
- **Site settings** (global `site-settings`): the site name, the links of the header and the text of the footer.

Pages and posts have drafts, autosave and at most 50 versions per document (`versions.maxPerDoc`: without it a document keeps a full copy of every array row on each save and the versions table grows without bound). Anyone reads a published document; a signed-in user also reads the drafts. SEO fields come from `@payloadcms/plugin-seo/fields`; the plugin itself only lends its translations (`seoTranslations` in the config), because it registers endpoints that generate field values, which nothing here uses.

Keep array fields small. The D1 adapter replaces an array by deleting its rows and inserting them again, one statement at a time and without a transaction, so a write that is interrupted halfway loses rows (payloadcms/payload#15219). That is why the blocks hold flat fields and the navigation of the site settings stops at 8 links.

## Bindings

`apps/web/wrangler.jsonc` binds `DB` (D1), `STORAGE` (R2, the media at the root of the bucket), `NEXT_INC_CACHE_R2_BUCKET` (the same bucket, for the page cache under `incremental-cache/`) and `NEXT_TAG_CACHE_D1` (the same database). `payload.config.ts` reads them two ways:

- In the deployed Worker, from `getCloudflareContext({ async: true })`.
- In every Node process (the Payload CLI, `next build`, `next dev`), from Wrangler's `getPlatformProxy`, over the local state in `.wrangler/v3` at the root of the repository: the directory the cron Worker and `wrangler d1` use too. It reaches your Cloudflare account only when `WRANGLER_REMOTE_BINDINGS=true` and the Wrangler config marks a binding `"remote": true`; the committed `wrangler.jsonc` marks none, because `next build` loads the config at module scope and would reach the account on every build. `{{runScript "db:migrate:remote"}}` and `{{runScript "ensure-admin:remote"}}` write a throwaway config where `DB` and `STORAGE` are remote, run for one command and delete it.

## Cache and revalidation

Pages are rendered on the first request and kept in R2 until a hook expires them, so a page view reads R2 and the tag cache, not D1.

- `[slug]` and `posts/[slug]` export `generateStaticParams` returning `[]` and `revalidate = false`: nothing is built ahead and nothing expires on a timer.
- `/`, `/posts` and `sitemap.xml` are `force-dynamic`. They have no dynamic segment to defer the first render to, and built ahead they would query D1 before the first migration: a first deploy would fail with `no such table`. Their data comes from `unstable_cache` with tags, so they still avoid D1.
- Every read of the site goes through `unstable_cache` with the tags `pages`, `posts`, `site-settings`, `page:<slug>` and `post:<slug>`. The `afterChange` and `afterDelete` hooks of the collections call `revalidateTag(tag, { expire: 0 })` for the tags of the document (the previous slug too after a rename) and of its list. A draft saved over a published document expires nothing. The site settings global expires its own tag.
- `revalidateTags` (`apps/web/src/lib/revalidate.ts`) ignores one error only: the plain `Error` Next.js throws when a write happens outside a request (a migration, the seed, `ensure-admin`), where there is no cache. Any other error is thrown again.
- A slug with no document answers 404 after `rememberMissing`, which gives the cached 404 the tag of the document it waits for (publishing that document replaces it at once) and a lifetime of 5 seconds. The cache has no queue, so it never renews an entry on a timer: the 404 of a made-up URL is replaced when that document is published.
- The OpenNext tag cache needs a `revalidations` table in D1. A migration creates it (`opennextjs-cloudflare preview` and `deploy` create it when it is missing, with the same columns).

Draft preview: `/next/preview?path=...&previewSecret=...` checks `PREVIEW_SECRET`, the signed-in Payload user and the path (`safeRedirectPath`), then turns draft mode on; draft reads skip the cache. The admin builds that address for the preview button and for live preview (`apps/web/src/lib/preview-url.ts`), and `RefreshRouteOnSave` refreshes the page as the editor types. `/next/exit-preview` turns draft mode off.

## Migrations

`push: false` on the adapter: the schema only changes through committed migrations, in development too, so the local and the production database never diverge.

1. Change a collection or the global.
2. `{{runScript "migrate:create" "<name>"}}` writes a migration and its `.json` snapshot into `apps/web/src/migrations/`. Read the SQL: it must be a delta. Commit the snapshot with it; without snapshots the command writes the whole schema again.
3. `{{runScript "migrate"}}` applies it to the local database.
4. `{{runScript "generate:types"}}` and `{{runScript "generate:importmap"}}` regenerate `src/payload-types.ts` and the admin import map. Commit both: they are generated, byte-identical to what Payload writes, and the linters ignore them.
5. To the production database: `{{runScript "db:migrate:remote"}}`, before the deploy that needs the new schema.

A D1 migration can print errors and still end with `Done`: check the tables afterwards (`wrangler d1 execute DB --local --command "PRAGMA table_list"`). Payload migrations are TypeScript, so `wrangler d1 migrations apply` cannot run them.

## Adding a collection

1. Write `apps/web/src/collections/<Name>.ts` and add it to `collections` in `payload.config.ts`. For a collection the site shows, add its cache tags to `cache-tags.ts`, its hooks to `hooks/revalidate.ts` and its reads to `lib/queries.ts`.
2. `{{runScript "generate:importmap"}}` if it uses a component Payload loads in the admin (a rich text field, a slug field).
3. `{{runScript "generate:types"}}`.
4. `{{runScript "migrate:create" "<name>"}}`, review, `{{runScript "migrate"}}`.

## D1 limits

- No transactions: the adapter's `beginTransaction` does nothing, so a failed hook does not roll a write back.
- 100 bound parameters per query: a bulk update or publish of about a hundred documents fails (payloadcms/payload#14766).
- A bulk delete can answer 500 and still delete everything.
- The admin preferences (the state of the sidebar, the columns of a list) are saved by the `onInit` hook of `payload.config.ts`: `@payloadcms/db-d1-sqlite` sets `upsert` to `updateOne` without `upsert: true`, so it never saves them (payloadcms/payload#17202).
- Routes answer 404 for about a minute after `wrangler deploy`: a smoke test retries.

